✅ ALL GIT COMMANDS (ONE-LINE + WHEN TO USE)
🔵 Repository Setup Commands
1. git init
Desc: Initialize a new local Git repository in a folder.
Use When: Starting version control for any project.

2. git remote add origin <URL>
Desc: Connect your local repo to a GitHub remote repo.
Use When: After creating a repo on GitHub and linking your project.

3. git clone <URL>
Desc: Download a remote repository to your system.
Use When: Starting work on an existing project.

4. git remote -v
Desc: View all remote repositories linked to your project.
Use When: To check remote URLs.

5. git remote remove <name>
Desc: Remove a remote repo link.
Use When: Wrong remote added or repo no longer valid.

6. git remote rename <old> <new>
Desc: Rename a remote.
Use When: Rename origin to something else.

7. git remote set-url <name> <newURL>
Desc: Change the URL of an existing remote.
Use When: Repo moved, URL changed, or switching HTTPS ↔ SSH.

8. git branch -r
Desc: Show all remote branches.
Use When: Checking remote repository branch list.

9. git fetch <remote> <branch>
Desc: Fetch only a specific branch.
Use When: You don’t want all branches.

10. git remote show origin
Desc: Show detailed info about the remote.
Use When: Check tracking branches, fetch info.

🔵 Staging, Committing, and Undoing
11. git add .
Desc: Stage all changed files.
Use When: Before committing all files.

12. git add <file>
Desc: Stage a single file.
Use When: Selectively preparing files for commit.

13. git commit -m "message"
Desc: Save staged changes with a message.
Use When: After staging changes.

14. git commit --amend
Desc: Modify the last commit message.
Use When: You typed the wrong commit message and haven’t pushed.

15. git reset <file>
Desc: Unstage a file but keep changes.
Use When: Used git add by mistake.

16. git checkout -- <file>
Desc: Discard changes in a file (not staged).
Use When: You want to undo local changes to a file.

17. git reset --hard
Desc: Discard all local changes.
Use When: Want a completely clean working directory.

🔵 Working With Branches
18. git branch
Desc: List all local branches.
Use When: See available branches.

19. git branch <branchname>
Desc: Create a new branch.
Use When: Start working on a new feature.

20. git switch <branch>
Desc: Move to another branch.
Use When: Change working branch.

21. git checkout <branch>
Desc: Alternative way to switch branches.
Use When: Older Git versions.

22. git checkout -b <branch>
Desc: Create and switch to a new branch.
Use When: Start a new feature immediately.

23. git branch -d <branch>
Desc: Delete a fully merged branch.
Use When: Branch merged and no longer needed.

24. git branch -D <branch>
Desc: Forcefully delete a branch (even if unmerged).
Use When: Discard unneeded or experimental branches.

25. git fetch --prune
Desc: Remove deleted remote branches from local.
Use When: Remote branch deleted but still showing.

🔵 Merging & Conflict Handling
26. git merge <branch>
Desc: Merge another branch into the current branch.
Use When: Combine completed feature branch into main.

27. git pull
Desc: Fetch + merge remote changes.
Use When: Update your code with remote repository.

28. git fetch
Desc: Download changes but do not merge.
Use When: Review changes before merging.

29. git rebase origin/main
Desc: Reapply your commits on top of the latest main.
Use When: Maintain clean and linear commit history.

🔵 Viewing Status & History
30. git status
Desc: Show current changes, staged files, branch.
Use When: Before staging or committing.

31. git log
Desc: Show commit history.
Use When: Review previous commits.

32. git log --oneline --graph
Desc: Clean, readable commit history.
Use When: Visualizing branch structure.

33. git diff
Desc: Show unstaged changes.
Use When: View what you changed before staging.

34. git blame <file>
Desc: Shows who changed each line of a file.
Use When: Finding who wrote/modified a specific line.

🔵 Stashing (Temporary Saving)
35. git stash
Desc: Save uncommitted changes temporarily.
Use When: Need to switch branches without committing.

36. git stash pop
Desc: Restore the last stashed changes and remove them from stash.
Use When: Return to work after switching back.

🔵 Remote Repository Commands
37. git push
Desc: Upload local commits to remote.
Use When: After committing changes.

38. git push -u origin <branch>
Desc: Push for first time and set upstream branch.
Use When: First push of new branch.

39. git push origin --delete <branch>
Desc: Delete a branch on remote.
Use When: Remote feature branch not needed anymore.

🔵 Other Important Commands
40. git config --global user.name "Your Name"
Desc: Set your name globally.
Use When: First-time Git setup.

41. git config --global user.email "you@example.com"
Desc: Set your email globally.
Use When: Needed for commits.

42. git rm --cached <file>
Desc: Remove a file from Git tracking but keep it locally.
Use When: Accidentally committed API keys, passwords, logs.

43. .gitignore file
Desc: Tells Git which files/folders NOT to track.
Use When: Hiding logs, node_modules, env, keys.
